# Monitriq uses TWO separate, gitignored env files — never one shared
# file — so the destructive local test harness can never touch whatever
# project the running app is pointed at. See docs/project/BUILD_STATE.md,
# "Environment separation".
#
# =============================================================================
# .env.local — the APPLICATION's own config (npm run dev / npm run build).
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in a real Supabase project's URL +
# anon key (e.g. "Monatriq Dev"). Never a service-role key here.
# =============================================================================

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# .env.test.local — the TEST HARNESS's own config (npm run test:*), a
# SEPARATE file. Copy this section to .env.test.local and point it at your
# LOCAL Docker Supabase stack ONLY ("supabase start" prints the URL/anon
# key; the local SERVICE_ROLE_KEY it prints goes under
# SUPABASE_TEST_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY below). The test harness
# (supabase/tests/shared/env.ts) hard-refuses to run against any URL that
# isn't 127.0.0.1/localhost, regardless of what's in this file — but keep
# it local-only regardless, since these suites create and delete real auth
# users and are run alongside `supabase db reset`.
#
# SUPABASE_TEST_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is used to create and clean up temporary
# auth fixture users so the isolation suites can run end-to-end. Named
# SUPABASE_TEST_* (not SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY) specifically so it's
# obvious at a glance this is not a production/application credential. The
# application itself never reads this variable, and it must never appear
# in .env.local. See docs/security/SECURITY_AND_RLS_PRINCIPLES.md #5/#12
# and supabase/tests/rls/README.md.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# SUPABASE_TEST_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
